This is one of the most common Spring AOP interview questions.

Many developers confuse Pointcut, Advice, and Around Advice.

Simple Analogy

Suppose there's a security guard at a company.

Pointcut = Which doors should be monitored?
Advice = What action should be performed?
Around Advice = A special action that can control the entire entry/exit process.
1. Pointcut

A Pointcut defines WHERE AOP should be applied.

Example:

Java
@Pointcut("execution(* com.example.service.*.*(..))")
public void serviceMethods() {}

This does nothing by itself.

It simply says:

Apply AOP to all methods in the service package.

Think Interview Answer
Plain Text
Pointcut = WHERE to apply AOP
2. Advice

An Advice defines WHAT action should be executed when the pointcut matches.

Examples:

Java
@Before("serviceMethods()")
public void logBefore() {
System.out.println("Before Method");
}
Java
@After("serviceMethods()")
public void logAfter() {
System.out.println("After Method");
}
Java
@AfterReturning("serviceMethods()")
Java
@AfterThrowing("serviceMethods()")
Think Interview Answer
Plain Text
Advice = WHAT action to perform
3. Around Advice

@Around is also an Advice, but it is the most powerful one.

Java
@Around("serviceMethods()")
public Object monitor(ProceedingJoinPoint jp)
throws Throwable {
 
System.out.println("Before");
 
Object result = jp.proceed();
 
System.out.println("After");
 
return result;
}

Output:

Plain Text
Before
Business Method
After

Unlike other advices, Around can:

✅ Execute before method

✅ Execute after method

✅ Skip method execution

✅ Modify return value

✅ Handle exceptions

✅ Measure execution time

