without @pointcut we can directly use @before right?
Exactly ✅

@Pointcut is optional.

You can directly write:

@Before("execution(* com.example.service.*.*(..))")
public void logBefore() {
    System.out.println("Before Method");
}
This works perfectly fine.

Why use @Pointcut then?

To avoid repeating the same expression multiple times.

Without Pointcut:

Java
@Before("execution(* com.example.service.*.*(..))")
 
@After("execution(* com.example.service.*.*(..))")
 
@AfterReturning("execution(* com.example.service.*.*(..))")
 
@AfterThrowing("execution(* com.example.service.*.*(..))")

Notice the same expression repeated 4 times.

With Pointcut
Java
@Pointcut("execution(* com.example.service.*.*(..))")
public void serviceMethods() {
}

Now reuse it:

Java
@Before("serviceMethods()")
public void before() {
}
 
@After("serviceMethods()")
public void after() {
}
 
@AfterReturning("serviceMethods()")
public void afterReturning() {
}
 
@AfterThrowing("serviceMethods()")
public void afterThrowing() {
}

Much cleaner.